- op: discuss  # 6298
  at: '2026-06-02T04:53:39Z'  # 6298
  by: agent  # 6298
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6298
  clock: 7  # 6298
  nonce: '6298'  # 6298
  message: "**Pass-24 finding (F24.3) — Zero-centrality extends beyond Python source to specific documentation files.**\n\n`sketch_precomputed.centrality_scores` is a dict mapping file path → centrality. Of 280 entries, 8 score exactly 0.0:\n\n```\nCHANGELOG.md\nCLAUDE.md\nGEMINI.md\ndocs/adr/0013-structured-tracker.md\ndocs/adr/0025-dispatch-publish-family-audits.md\ndocs/adr/0026-ipc-family-audit.md\ndocs/hypergumbo-spec.md\npackages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/taint_sanitizers/encryption.yaml\n```\n\nZero-rate by extension: `.md` 7/111 (6.3%), `.yaml` 1/167 (0.6%). Most md/yaml files DO get nonzero scores; these 8 are specifically not-imported-by-anything within the analyzed file graph.\n\nINV-napap covers the Python-source-files-excluded case (0 of 838 .py files). Pass-24 finding is adjacent: even within INCLUDED extensions, key documentation files (CHANGELOG, spec, GEMINI/CLAUDE/CONTRIBUTING) score 0 because nothing imports them. The centrality scoring uses graph-import structure as input — for files only referenced via URL, PR description, or human convention (CHANGELOG mentioned in commits; spec.md mentioned in agent_notes; etc.), the score is 0.\n\nConsumer asking \"what are this repo's most important files?\" gets a misleading answer: CHANGELOG.md at 0.0 vs `docs/hypergumbo-self-catalog/user_cache_sinks.yaml` at 7.42 (top score).\n\n**Significance.** Two faces of the same root cause: the centrality input signal is \"what's imported by other analyzed files.\" For Python files INV-napap shows zero coverage (likely a Python-specific bug). For docs, the zero scores are correct-by-definition but misleading by intent — they reflect \"not-imported-anywhere\" rather than \"not-important.\"\n\nPossible resolution: either (a) clarify the field's semantic (\"intra-graph import centrality only — files not imported anywhere get 0 even if project-critical\"), or (b) widen the input signal (track URL references in code/agent_notes; track CHANGELOG/AGENTS/spec as automatic high-centrality anchors). Documentation fix is cheaper.\n\n**Lab notebook entry:** `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_06022026_0250_pass24.md`."  # 6298
- op: discuss  # 77bc
  at: '2026-06-02T05:27:58Z'  # 77bc
  by: agent  # 77bc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 77bc
  clock: 8  # 77bc
  nonce: 77bc  # 77bc
  message: "Pass-25 cross-format consistency probe — INV-napap's centrality_scores-vs-source disjunction has a structural cause:\n\nF25.2 (sharper measurement): centrality_scores keys (280 entries) and file-kind node paths (901 entries) are **disjoint by file extension** — CS contains 111 .md + 167 .yaml + 1 .toml + 1 .json (all docs/config); file-nodes are 844 .py + 11 .sh + 8 .ts + 25 noext + 1 each (yaml/html/js/go/rs/swift/json) — all source code. Pairwise path intersection = 0.\n\nF25.15 (structural cause): 11 ARs have files_analyzed > 0 but emit 0 unique-path nodes:\n  markdown: 113 files → 0 path-bearing nodes (but ~111 → centrality_scores)\n  json: 9 / toml: 9 / route-handler-linker: 8 / ipc-linker: 9 / js-module-linker: 5 / gitignore: 3\n  sql / xml / wgsl / go-cobra-linker: 1 each\n\nThe markdown analyzer scans 113 markdown files and contributes 0 file-kind nodes BUT 111 entries to centrality_scores. Centrality_scores has its own input pipeline disjoint from the node graph. INV-napap's framing (\"centrality_scores excludes Python source\") is correct but the deeper mechanism is broader: markdown/yaml/toml/json analyzers produce centrality entries without producing file-kind nodes; source-language analyzers (python, bash, typescript, ...) produce file-kind nodes without producing centrality entries. Two analyzer cohorts, two output surfaces, zero overlap.\n\nThis makes INV-napap's \"centrality excludes Python source\" a particular instance of \"centrality has its own analyzer cohort.\" The structural fix isn't \"add Python source to centrality\" — it's deciding which analyzers feed centrality_scores and whether source-file centrality is a separate input layer. INV-jifus (sketch_precomputed has no schema) is the natural home for the schema decision; INV-napap's scope can extend to \"centrality_scores excludes source-language files entirely\" (broader than just Python).\n\nPass-24 F24.3 noted CHANGELOG.md / spec.md / 5 ADRs / 1 yaml score 0.0 — pass-25 explains: these are the markdown/yaml entries CS does score, just at value 0 because nothing imports them. The 0-score-on-docs vs missing-source split is two faces of the same underlying \"wrong analyzer cohort\" issue."  # 77bc
- op: discuss  # 8a78
  at: '2026-06-02T05:47:42Z'  # 8a78
  by: agent  # 8a78
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8a78
  clock: 9  # 8a78
  nonce: 8a78  # 8a78
  message: "Pass-26 producer-consumer trace probes — sketch_precomputed / supply_chain_summary derivation rules undocumented at the schema layer.\n\nF26.8 (centrality_scores producer attribution opaque): centrality_scores has 280 entries (111 .md + 167 .yaml + 1 .toml + 1 .json). No record-level provenance fields exist on CS entries. Producer attribution must be inferred via paths-vs-AR-claims and the inference doesn't reconcile:\n\n- markdown AR: files_analyzed=113; CS .md count = 111. Plausible producer (delta 2 unaccounted).\n- yaml_ansible AR: files_analyzed=0; CS .yaml count = 167. Producer mismatch — yaml_ansible analyzed 0 files but CS has 167 yaml entries. Some OTHER pass (probably the tree-sitter `yaml` pass, or `properties` AR, or a sketch-side yaml walker) contributes those entries.\n- toml AR: files_analyzed=9; CS .toml = 1. Producer attribution unclear — toml AR sees 9 files but only 1 reaches CS.\n- json AR: files_analyzed=9; CS .json = 1. Same pattern.\n\nCentrality_scores has its own analyzer pipeline disjoint from the node graph (also surfaced as pass-25 F25.2/F25.15). Pass-26 sharpens: no analyzer's files_analyzed cleanly maps to CS contribution count except markdown (113→111). The schema-level fix would be to either (a) carry a per-entry `source_pass_id` field on CS records, or (b) document the pipeline (sketch.py reads which files? via what walker? feeds CS via which transform?) in spec.\n\nF26.2/F26.3/F26.4 (supply_chain_summary derivation rules undocumented): scs counts files and symbols per tier, but the file-counting rule diverges from naive expectation:\n\n```\ntier            scs.files  derived.unique_path  derived.file_kind_unique_path\nfirst_party     914        914                  901\ninternal_dep    1          0                    0\nexternal_dep    10         8                    0\nderived_skipped 0          0                    0\n```\n\n- first_party: scs.files=914 matches unique paths across ALL first_party nodes (not just file-kind). 13 of those 914 paths come from non-file-kind nodes (method/function/class) whose path is included but isn't represented by a file-kind node. Subtle: scs.files counts unique paths, not unique file-kind nodes.\n- internal_dep: scs.files=1 is a phantom — 0 first-party paths exist in the substrate for the 486 internal_dep symbols. Where does the count of 1 come from? Likely a single import-target file represented only at edge-source level. Producer rule undocumented.\n- external_dep: scs.files=10 vs 8 derived paths — 2 phantom files. The 1236 external_dep symbols mostly have path='<external>' (sentinel) and the 8 derived paths are the workspace npm packages.\n\nThese are derivation-rule gaps: the spec doesn't say how scs.<tier>.files is computed, so consumers can't predict the count or interpret deltas. The fix is either schema-level documentation of the derivation OR per-entry provenance (which path contributed).\n\nINV-napap's existing scope is centrality_scores specifically; INV-jifus is the broader \"sketch_precomputed has no schema\" parent. Both umbrella the pass-26 findings. The cumulative pattern across pass-24 / pass-25 / pass-26 is that sketch_precomputed is a high-information surface (centrality, vocabulary, readme_description, config_info, plus implicit pipelines) with weak schema discipline — derivation rules are tribal knowledge in sketch.py rather than wire-format contract."  # 8a78
- op: discuss  # 9d05
  at: '2026-06-03T00:12:55Z'  # 9d05
  by: agent  # 9d05
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9d05
  clock: 10  # 9d05
  nonce: 9d05  # 9d05
  message: "Pass-40 F40.C1+C4 batch — markdown analyzer skips file-kind anchor emission entirely.\n\nF40.C1: Generated /tmp/p40_docs_bm.json via 'hypergumbo run docs --include-docs' on docs/ (108 files, 92 .md). Result: 2861 nodes, node-kind breakdown {section:1768, link:502, code_block:497, external_symbol:92, file:2}. The 2 file-kind nodes are both ansible (YAML). **ZERO file-kind nodes for the 92 .md files.** The markdown analyzer emits section/link/code_block synthesized symbols but never a file-kind anchor symbol per markdown file. Distinct from INV-napap's original framing of 'centrality_scores excludes Python source files' — there it was absence-of-scoring; here it's absence-of-emission.\n\nConsumer impact: per-file aggregation queries (centrality-by-file, tier-by-file, supply_chain by file) cannot enumerate markdown files at all from a substrate. centrality_scores, supply_chain_summary, file-language histograms all silently exclude markdown content. The 92 markdown files exist as 2861 internal section/link/code_block nodes only.\n\nF40.C4 (sibling): all 2861 DOCS nodes report supply_chain_tier='?' (key absent from dict). The tier classifier doesn't classify markdown nodes at all. Sibling of INV-nuzas (mis-labels first-party as external) and INV-napap original (centrality_scores excludes Python files). Here it's complete absence for markdown kind — neither tier emitted nor file-kind anchor to attach tier to.\n\nFix path: emit a file-kind anchor symbol for each analyzed file regardless of language (same fix path as INV-napap original). Tier classifier should default to first_party for nodes that have a non-<external> path. The markdown analyzer scaffolding gap is broader than centrality and tier — affects supply_chain_summary, file enumeration, centrality_scores, and tier classification simultaneously."  # 9d05
